From a9967391bc2f937aabc3837f3015f557f1ee3fca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 08:36:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/50] DucoBoxSerial: add native DucoBox ventilation plugin

Talks to a DucoBox Focus/Silent over its serial CLI and publishes each node as
Domoticz devices: temperature, humidity, CO2, airflow percentage and the derived
flow, box fan speed and power draw, switch state, override mode and countdown,
plus the setpoints and thresholds that can be written back.

Device identity is a pure function of node id and capability. It must not depend
on runtime state, because an id that can change is a device Domoticz silently
recreates -- stranding the original under its name and splitting the history
between them.

The box is a slow single-master bus that answers one command at a time, and most
of what follows exists because of that.

Replies are read from their own command's echo onwards. The box echoes what it
received, so anything ahead of that echo belongs to an earlier command still
arriving. Reading the whole buffer let one command answer another: raw humidity
in centi-percent was published as CO2 in ppm, and a write the box had confirmed
was reported as rejected because a leftover node table happened to contain the
word "Failed".

A quiet window is held before each command, two seconds either side of the node
table, so a previous reply arrives before the buffer is drained rather than just
after it.

Budgets are per command and were measured, not chosen: 20s for the two commands
that dump a node table, which take 10-12s, and 10s for everything else, where the
slowest success observed was 4.4s. Retrying cannot help a reply that is merely
slow, so the table reads get one or two attempts rather than three.

A command whose read window closes having received nothing at all is counted, and
at three in a row the bus is declared silent: the cycle is abandoned and the port
recycled rather than spending a budget on every remaining command. Any byte
clears the count, since a partial or garbled reply is what the retries are for.

Which spelling of the fan commands a given firmware answers to is learned once
and then used alone. It is forgotten only when the box explicitly rejects it,
never on a timeout -- a struggling bus is no evidence the name is wrong.

Node serials are validated by shape and merged rather than replaced. That column
carries status words as well as identifiers, and storing one of those as a serial
was enough to flap the topology signature and re-run the startup probe on every
cycle.

The startup sensor probe and the first configuration read are kept in separate
cycles, since each walks every node, and the config read cannot be postponed
indefinitely.

Diagnostics say how a command failed rather than only that it did, because the
cases want different fixes and are otherwise indistinguishable: no echo means it
never reached the box, an echo without a completion marker means the reply was
cut off by the budget, and a marker without the expected token means the box
answered something else. A command that succeeds using more than half its budget
is logged with its duration, so the budgets stay measured.

Published values are range-checked, and on an RH-capable node, either its
humidity or its temperature latching bit-exact for a sustained window is
treated as a hardware fault rather than data: the node is cycled through its
full range, a minute at 100%, a minute at 0%, then a minute at 10%, before
being released back to Auto -- the serial equivalent of the physical button
press known to unstick it. A single step toward one end and back was tried
first and confirmed live to fire correctly without ever clearing the fault;
the full swing is what the button press actually does. Repeat attempts on
the same node are gated behind a 45-minute cooldown: a longer one left a
chronically re-latching node sitting visibly stuck for up to an hour between
attempts, even though a single attempt completes in under three minutes and
the node routinely re-shows a bit-exact value well before the cooldown is
up. A latched temperature also needs to already be outside the plausible
indoor range before it counts, since unlike humidity a real room can sit at
one reading for a long time without anything being wrong.

